1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,450
This is Terra Strike: an Amiga game, written by Claude Code, and tested live through Amiga DevBench.

2
00:00:07,450 --> 00:00:18,500
It started with one request: a Zarch-style landscape shooter for the sixty-eight K Amiga, with tanks, buildings and ships to destroy, unit tests, and testing through devbench.

3
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:29,050
Claude began by talking to the Amiga over M C P: a ping, a task list, and a look at the existing games, to reuse their 3D maths and music player.

4
00:00:29,050 --> 00:00:41,100
The game logic is portable C, with sixty-seven unit tests that run on the Mac, on the Amiga, and inside the game. The first run caught a real bug: enemies firing every frame.

5
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:51,100
Devbench cross-compiles in Docker, deploys to the emulator, and launches the game. First frame on the Amiga. But only five frames a second.

6
00:00:51,100 --> 00:01:03,550
So Claude exposed timers as bridge variables, and read them live. A hundred and fifty-nine milliseconds were going on area fills. A hand-written span filler took it to twenty-seven frames a second.

7
00:01:03,550 --> 00:01:16,149
Then it played the game. Key presses are injected straight into the Amiga's input device. Return starts the game, holding cursor up lifts off, and the take-off event arrives in the remote log.

8
00:01:16,149 --> 00:01:26,369
Screenshots exposed bugs the tests couldn't: cracks at the screen edge, and flickering colours. A palette read showed the blitter was clearing the buffer still on screen.

9
00:01:26,369 --> 00:01:36,369
A scene hook drops the craft straight into a tank battle. Hold fire, and the score comes back over the bridge. One hundred. First kill.

10
00:01:36,369 --> 00:01:47,020
Running the tests on the Amiga uncovered two bugs in devbench itself. Once those were fixed, all sixty-seven passed on the emulated sixty-eight oh sixty.

11
00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:59,020
Underneath, devbench speaks a simple line protocol to the bridge daemon. Here is the first kill, verbatim: the hook, the fire key, and the Amiga reporting the kill.

12
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:11,620
The end-to-end test turned up a mystery: the wrong score. A second game, started by the Amiga's launcher, had grabbed our key presses and variable names. Claude tracked it down, and every check passed.

13
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:24,620
For the main screen, Claude wrote an image prompt, ChatGPT painted it, and Claude converted it to thirty-two Amiga colours, choosing a palette that keeps the logo and the craft intact.

14
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:36,620
Each screen loads its own palette. Devbench checks the colour registers and the on-screen text, and a key press takes us into the game.

15
00:02:36,620 --> 00:02:49,620
And here it is: played entirely by Claude, through devbench key injection and scene hooks.

16
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:56,620
One session. Under forty minutes from a prompt to a tested, playable Amiga game.

